## Before you claim

Check that you:

- are too ill to work, or can only do a little work
- are under State Pension age
- have paid, or been credited with, enough **National Insurance**, usually in the last 2 to 3 tax years
- are not getting Statutory Sick Pay (you can claim ESA up to 3 months before it ends)

If you are not sure about your National Insurance, you can check your record on GOV.UK.

## What you need

- your **National Insurance number**
- your **bank or building society details**
- your **GP's or doctor's** name and address
- a **fit note** if you have been ill for more than 7 days
- details of any income from **pensions** or **insurance**
- details of any work you are doing

## How to claim

1. **Apply online** at GOV.UK, or call the **Universal Credit helpline** on **0800 328 5644** if you cannot apply online.
2. **Send your fit note.** Keep sending new ones until the DWP tells you to stop.
3. **Go to your first appointment** with a work coach, if you are asked.
4. **Fill in the ESA50 form** about how your health affects you.
5. **Have your Work Capability Assessment**, unless you are told you do not need one.
6. **Get your decision.** You are paid at the basic rate while you wait.

Read [the Work Capability Assessment explained](/benefits/esa/work-capability-assessment/).

## Claiming with Universal Credit

You can get **New Style ESA and Universal Credit at the same time**. Universal Credit counts ESA as income, so your Universal Credit goes down by the same amount. It is still worth claiming both, because:

- New Style ESA is not affected by your savings or partner's income
- it gives you National Insurance credits
- if your Universal Credit stops, you may still get ESA

Find out about the [Universal Credit health element](/benefits/universal-credit-health/).

## Also check

Being ill or disabled can bring extra costs. You may also be able to get:

- [PIP](/benefits/pip/), for the extra costs of a disability (try our free PIP self-check)
- [help with NHS health costs](/support/health-costs/), such as prescriptions and dental care
- [money off bills](/support/money-off-bills/), such as council tax and energy
